        "content": "{\n  \"chapter_proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"chapter_id\": \"CH02\",\n      \"title\": \"微生物特征：预测 ICI 反应的菌分类群与代谢物\",\n      \"purpose\": \"系统评估与实体瘤 ICI 疗效相关的微生物分类学标志物及代谢物特征，区分观察性关联与定量证据，明确生物标志物的情境依赖性。\",\n      \"scope\": \"涵盖人类临床队列中的菌群组成（门/属/种/株）及代谢物（SCFA、肌苷等）数据；排除仅动物模型无人类数据支持的特征；明确区分粪便/血浆与肿瘤组织内浓度证据。\",\n      \"substantive_threads\": [\n        {\n          \"theme\": \"预测范式的演变：从单一菌种到生态功能\",\n          \"argument\": \"早期研究聚焦单一有益菌（如 A. muciniphila），但跨队列复现性低；近期证据支持菌株水平（P0015, P0179）及功能通路（P0097, P0496）比分类学丰度更具预测稳定性。\",\n          \"sources\": {\n            \"redacted\": true,\n            \"original_bytes\": 45,\n            \"sha256\": \"5fe00853049482f400a29370e986209e92c5e105d679513575094d4effb69d2d\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"theme\": \"跨癌种的一致性菌属与情境特异性\",\n          \"argument\": \"Akkermansia、Faecalibacterium 在黑色素瘤/NSCLC/RCC 中常与响应正相关（P0298, P0120），但 Bacteroides 等菌属作用存在癌种及 ICI 类型依赖性（P0130, P0170）；人群地理与饮食背景显著修饰标志物效力（P0545, P0478）。\",\n          \"sources\": {\n            \"redacted\": true,\n            \"original_bytes\": 63,\n            \"sha256\": \"3afa519fcd908977dcf731818505a0a180a653174e578fd05c88b8648dbef6e8\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"theme\": \"代谢物定量证据：SCFA 与肌苷的血浆/粪便数据 vs. 肿瘤组织缺口\",\n          \"argument\": \"粪便/血浆 SCFA 浓度（如丙酸）与 PFS 正相关且有定量数据支持（P0092, P0223）；肌苷在人类血浆中与 OS 相关且机制明确（P0582, P0587），但人类肿瘤微环境内肌苷及 SCFA 的直接浓度测量仍缺失，限制机制外推。\",\n          \"sources\": {\n            \"redacted\": true,\n            \"original_bytes\": 54,\n            \"sha256\": \"3400a79c7b51f025413f1b814cb4483d34aff0caee7c6182f8425858c2d911b3\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"theme\": \"方法学异质性与标志物泛化挑战\",\n          \"argument\": \"16S 与宏基因组测序差异、采样时间点（基线 vs. 纵向）、及混杂因素（抗生素/PPI）导致标志物不一致；需转向多组学整合及标准化流程（P0354, P0565）。\",\n          \"sources\": {\n            \"redacted\": true,\n            \"original_bytes\": 45,\n            \"sha256\": \"8e71090ed1adccfb5576e59af26334857809ae2211fc1c258e34fce227fb47e9\"\n          }\n        }\n      ],\n      \"excluded_source_handles\": [],\n      \"supplement_requests\": [\n        {\n          \"gap_id\": \"intratumoral_metabolite_quant\",\n          \"gap_question\": \"What are the measured physiological concentrations of inosine and SCFAs inside the human tumour microenvironment correlating with ICI response?\",\n          \"success_criteria\": \"Identify prospective human studies reporting absolute concentrations (e.g., μmol/g or μM) of inosine or specific SCFAs in paired tumor tissue and serum/stool from ICI-treated solid tumor patients.\",\n          \"intended_use\": \"To explicitly define the evidence gap between systemic metabolite levels (plasma/stool) and local tumor microenvironment concentrations in the review's limitation section.\",\n          \"targeted_queries\": [\n            \"human tumor tissue metabolite concentration inosine immune checkpoint inhibitor\",\n            \"intratumoral short-chain fatty acids quantification cancer immunotherapy patients\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"gap_id\": \"strain_level_functional_validation\",\n          \"gap_question\": \"Are there human clinical studies validating strain-specific microbial functions (e.g., specific Bifidobacterium strains) rather than species-level abundance for ICI prediction?\",\n          \"success_criteria\": \"Find human cohort studies using strain-level resolution (metagenomics/culturomics) linked to ICI outcomes, distinguishing between different strains of the same species.\",\n          \"intended_use\": \"To support the argument that strain-level resolution is necessary for robust biomarkers and to assess the readiness of live biotherapeutic products.\",\n          \"targeted_queries\": [\n            \"strain-level gut microbiome biomarker immune checkpoint inhibitor human cohort\",\n            \"specific bacterial strain function immune checkpoint inhibitor response clinical study\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"directed_reads\": [\n        \"P0092\",\n        \"P0582\",\n        \"P0298\",\n        \"P0170\",\n        \"P0478\",\n        \"P0015\",\n        \"P0545\"\n      ],\n      \"transition_logic\": \"承接 CH01 确立的临床关联（抗生素时机、FMT 试验），本章具体化‘哪些微生物’在起作用，为 CH03 的机制阐释（‘如何起作用’）提供物质基础；强调分类学标志物的异质性将自然引出 CH03 对功能通路和分子机制的深入探讨。\",\n      \"conditions_and_limitations\": \"多数菌属 - 疗效关联为观察性数据，不能推断因果；代谢物定量数据主要限于血浆/粪便，肿瘤组织内浓度数据缺失；标志物效力受癌种、ICI 类型、地理及饮食背景显著修饰，不可泛化为通用标准。\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"screened_sources\": []\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants a chapter proposal for Chapter 2 (CH02) of a review on the gut microbiome and immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) in solid tumors.\nThe chapter title is \"微生物特征：预测 ICI 反应的菌分类群与代谢物\" (Microbial Features: Taxa and Metabolites Predicting ICI Response).\nThe goal is to identify specific microbial taxa and metabolites associated with ICI response or resistance in solid tumors.\nI need to structure the chapter proposal with:\n- chapter_id\n- title\n- purpose\n- scope\n- substantive_threads (ordered themes)\n- excluded_source_handles (if any)\n- supplement_requests (gap questions)\n- directed_reads (specific sources to focus on)\n- transition logic (how it connects to CH01 and CH03)\n- conditions and limitations (evidence strength, context)\n\nKey constraints from the system prompt:\n- Do not write a Q&A list.\n- Organize by argument主线 (argumentative thread), not paper-by-paper summary.\n- Distinguish association vs. causation, mechanism vs. observation.\n- Retain source metadata (source_handle).\n- Do not overclaim (e.g., no III期 RCTs exist).\n- Address the specific scope adjustment: metabolite quantitative data is partial (plasma/fecal SCFA exists, but intratumoral inosine/SCFA is missing).\n- Use the provided source_routing ledger.\n- JSON only.\n\nPlan for CH02:\n1.  **Introduction/Thesis**: Move from \"single bug\" hunting to \"functional/ecological\" signatures. Acknowledge heterogeneity across cancer types.\n2.  **Taxonomic Signatures**: Discuss consistent genera (Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium, Faecalibacterium) vs. context-dependent ones (Bacteroides). Highlight strain-level vs. species-level resolution.\n3.  **Metabolite Signatures**: Focus on SCFAs (fecal/plasma quantitative data available) and Inosine (mechanistic link strong, human intratumoral data missing). Distinguish systemic vs. local concentrations.\n4.  **Methodological Heterogeneity**: Address why signatures vary (sequencing depth, geography, diet, ICI type).\n5.  **Transition**: Link to CH03 (mechanisms) by noting that taxonomy implies function, but metabolites are the direct effectors.\n\nSources to prioritize:\n- Quantitative SCFA data: P0092 (Nomura et al. 2020), P0223.\n- Inosine mechanism/association: P0582, P0587, P0162.\n- Taxonomic signatures (Akkermansia, etc.): P0021, P0023, P0062, P0120, P0146, P0298 (Meta-analysis).\n- Strain/Functional level: P0015, P0097, P0179, P0496.\n- Heterogeneity/Context: P0170 (Enterotypes), P0478 (Cross-cohort), P0545 (Population-specific).\n- Intratumoral vs. Gut: P0019, P0163, P0233, P0535.\n\nGap to address in supplement_requests:\n- Intratumoral metabolite concentrations (Inosine/SCFA) in humans.\n- Standardized strain-level functional validation.\n\nLet's draft the JSON structure.\n- chapter_id: CH02\n- title: 微生物特征：预测 ICI 反应的菌分类群与代谢物\n- purpose: 系统评估与实体瘤 ICI 疗效相关的微生物分类学标志物及代谢物特征，区分观察性关联与定量证据，明确生物标志物的情境依赖性。\n- scope: 涵盖人类临床队列中的菌群组成（门/属/种/株）及代谢物（SCFA、肌苷等）数据；排除仅动物模型无人类数据支持的特征；明确区分粪便/血浆与肿瘤组织内浓度证据。\n- substantive_threads:\n    1.  从单一菌种到生态功能：预测范式的演变 (From single taxa to ecological function).\n    2.  跨癌种的一致性菌属与情境特异性 (Consistent genera vs. context-specificity).\n    3.  代谢物定量证据：SCFA 与肌苷的血浆/粪便数据 vs. 肿瘤组织缺口 (Metabolite quantitative evidence).\n    4.  方法学异质性与标志物泛化挑战 (Methodological heterogeneity).\n- excluded_source_handles: None (use routing).\n- supplement_requests: Need to ask for intratumoral metabolite concentrations.\n- directed_reads: P0092, P0582, P0298, P0170, P0478.\n- transition logic: CH01 establishes clinical associations (antibiotics/FMT); CH02 details *what* specific microbes/metabolites are involved; CH03 explains *how* they work (mechanisms).\n\nRefining the argument:\nThe chapter should argue that while taxonomic signatures (e.g., Akkermansia) are promising, they are inconsistent across cohorts due to methodological and biological heterogeneity. Functional signatures (metabolites like SCFAs, Inosine) offer a more robust link, but human quantitative data is largely limited to systemic circulation (plasma/stool), not the tumor microenvironment (TME), creating a translational gap.\n\nEnsure I don't overclaim causality for observational data.\nEnsure I distinguish between fecal/plasma SCFA (available) and intratumoral (missing).\nEnsure I mention strain-level resolution (P0015, P0179).\n\nLet's construct the JSON.",
